 # Visualization and Rendering Techniques
 
-Also called: Isosurface Rendering, Multi-Isosurface Rendering, Optimized Mesh Reference, Triangular Mesh Scene Rendering
 
 ## Overview
-This family encompasses methods for visualizing isosurfaces extracted from volumetric scalar fields, ranging from direct procedural ray-marching to explicit rasterization of pre-computed meshes. Unlike full volumetric rendering which accumulates light through the entire volume, these techniques isolate specific density thresholds to reveal internal structures or material boundaries [S4, unofficial][S2]. The primary distinction among siblings lies in the representation: [Isosurface Rendering](7669-isosurface-rendering.md) uses procedural ray marching for smooth, continuous appearance without explicit geometry, while [Triangular Mesh Scene Rendering](4644-triangular-mesh-scene-rendering.md) and [Optimized Mesh Reference](1888-optimized-mesh-reference.md) rely on explicit triangle meshes for standard rasterization [S1, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. [Multi-Isosurface Rendering](8745-multi-isosurface-rendering.md) extends this by extracting and managing a subset of representative surfaces at different isovalue levels for interactive exploration [S2].
+This family covers methods for visualizing isosurfaces extracted from volumetric scalar fields, ranging from direct procedural ray-marching to explicit rasterization of pre-computed meshes. Unlike full volumetric rendering which accumulates light through the entire volume, these techniques isolate specific density thresholds to reveal internal structures or material boundaries [G]. The primary distinction among siblings lies in the representation: [Isosurface Rendering](7669-isosurface-rendering.md) uses procedural ray marching for smooth, continuous appearance without explicit geometry, while [Triangular Mesh Scene Rendering](4644-triangular-mesh-scene-rendering.md) and [Optimized Mesh Reference](1888-optimized-mesh-reference.md) rely on explicit triangle meshes for standard rasterization. [Multi-Isosurface Rendering](8745-multi-isosurface-rendering.md) extends this by extracting and managing a subset of representative surfaces at different isovalue levels for interactive exploration.
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family operate on a discretized domain, typically a regular grid (Cartesian, octree, or tetrahedral) where each cell contains scalar values or occupancy states at its vertices or centers. The core mechanism involves traversing the grid cells and evaluating the data to determine which parts of the domain constitute the surface [G].
-
-1.  **Domain Traversal**: The algorithm iterates over the volume data structure [G]. For regular grids, this often involves checking each cell or voxel [G].
-2.  **Surface Determination**: A criterion is applied to identify surface locations. In isosurface methods, this involves comparing scalar values against a threshold (isovalue) to detect sign changes or equality [30-isosurface-extraction.md]. In blocky meshing, this involves checking occupancy states or material IDs to identify exposed boundaries [31-blocky-meshing.md].
-3.  **Primitive Generation**: Geometric primitives (vertices, edges, faces) are generated based on the determined surface locations [G].
-4.  **Mesh Coherence**: Adjacent primitives are merged or connected to form a coherent mesh, often requiring vertex merging to avoid duplicates and ensure watertightness [30-isosurface-extraction.md].
+All methods in this family operate on a discretized domain, typically a regular grid (Cartesian, octree, or tetrahedral) where each cell contains scalar values or occupancy states at its vertices or centers [G]. For detailed traversal, surface determination, and mesh coherence mechanisms, see [Surface extraction](29-surface-extraction.md).
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Optimized Mesh Reference](1888-optimized-mesh-reference.md)
-A theoretical performance baseline for indirect isosurface visualization, representing the best-case scenario where an explicit mesh is rendered with zero overhead for geometry extraction or occlusion culling. It serves to measure the theoretical upper bound of rendering performance by evaluating a highly-optimized explicit mesh representation, allowing researchers to assess how close a proposed pipeline comes to the ideal efficiency of forward mesh rendering. Unlike direct ray-marching references, it isolates the rasterization cost of an already-extracted and optimized surface.
+A theoretical performance baseline for indirect isosurface visualization, representing the best-case scenario where geometry extraction and occlusion culling are instantaneous. It measures the rendering time of a highly-optimized explicit mesh representation, excluding the costs of extraction and visibility determination. It serves as an upper bound for forward mesh rendering efficiency, allowing researchers to assess how close a practical pipeline comes to the theoretical limit of rendering an optimized isosurface. Choose it to benchmark the rasterization efficiency of an optimized isosurface pipeline.
 
 ### [Triangular Mesh Scene Rendering](4644-triangular-mesh-scene-rendering.md)
-A method for rendering a scene composed of a list of triangular mesh objects, allowing multiple contour surfaces or related data sets to be displayed in a single plot. It differs from volume rendering methods by operating directly on explicit geometric primitives (triangles) rather than sampling a scalar field, enabling the visualization of multiple distinct isosurfaces or datasets simultaneously. The primary trade-off is that standard back-to-front rasterization engines may render intersecting surfaces with visible artifacts (ragged edges), whereas engines with proper depth buffering handle intersections correctly.
+A method for rendering a scene composed of a list of triangular mesh objects, allowing multiple contour surfaces or related data sets to be displayed in a single plot. It differs from volume rendering methods by operating directly on explicit geometric primitives (triangles) rather than sampling a scalar field, enabling the visualization of multiple distinct isosurfaces or datasets simultaneously. The primary trade-off is that standard back-to-front rasterization engines may render intersecting surfaces with visible artifacts (ragged edges), whereas engines with proper depth buffering handle intersections correctly. Choose it when visualizing multiple distinct isosurfaces or datasets simultaneously, provided the rendering engine supports proper depth buffering.
 
 ### [Isosurface Rendering](7669-isosurface-rendering.md)
-Extracts and visualizes a specific density threshold (isovalue) from a volumetric dataset, rendering only the surface where the scalar field equals that threshold. Unlike polygonal mesh extraction (e.g., Marching Cubes) which generates discrete geometry, this method typically employs ray marching to procedurally locate the surface at render time, producing a smooth, continuous appearance without explicit polygonal artifacts. It is distinct from full volumetric rendering because it ignores internal structures and occluded surfaces, relying on lighting calculations to convey shape and depth.
+Extracts and visualizes a specific density threshold (isovalue) from a volumetric dataset, rendering only the surface where the scalar field equals that threshold. Unlike polygonal mesh extraction (e.g., Marching Cubes) which generates discrete geometry, this method typically employs ray marching to procedurally locate the surface at render time, producing a smooth, continuous appearance without explicit polygonal artifacts. It is distinct from full volumetric rendering because it ignores internal structures and occluded surfaces, relying on lighting calculations to convey shape and depth. Choose it when a smooth, continuous appearance is desired without the overhead of explicit mesh generation, and internal structures are not required.
 
 ### [Multi-Isosurface Rendering](8745-multi-isosurface-rendering.md)
-Visualizes the distribution of specific scalar values within a volumetric dataset by extracting and rendering surfaces where the scalar field equals a constant isovalue ($f(x, y, z) = \text{const}$). Unlike Direct Volume Rendering (DVR), which accumulates light along rays to show internal structures, MIR isolates distinct material boundaries or density levels as explicit triangle meshes. It is distinguished from single-isosurface methods by extracting a representative subset of isosurfaces at uniformly chosen isovalues, allowing users to interactively explore multiple internal structures simultaneously or sequentially.
